const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens deliberately deceive other ravens?', a: 'Yes — ravens perform behaviours that function to create false beliefs in competitors. The clearest examples involve caching: ravens observed caching food will cache it again (recache) elsewhere when they know a competitor has seen the original cache. Some ravens perform fake caching — going through the motions of caching food (digging, covering) without actually placing food — apparently to mislead competitors into wasting time searching a false location. These behaviours suggest ravens model others\' mental states (theory of mind) and act to manipulate those states.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens more deceptive than crows?', a: 'Deceptive caching and false caching behaviours have been documented in both ravens and in Western Scrub-Jays (now California Scrub-Jays). The scrub-jay research by Clayton\'s group was the first rigorous experimental demonstration; Bugnyar\'s parallel work documented similar behaviours in ravens. It is not established that ravens are more deceptive than crows or jays — all these corvids show evidence of tactical deception in caching contexts, suggesting it is a corvid family-level capability rather than unique to ravens.' },
  { q: 'What is a "fake cache" in ravens?', a: 'A fake cache (or "false cache", "empty cache") is when a raven goes through the physical motions of caching food — pecking at the ground, pushing substrate over the cache site, adopting the characteristic cache-covering posture — but does not actually deposit any food. The purpose appears to be misdirection: a watching competitor who follows the caching raven will find nothing at the false cache site, while the real food has been hidden elsewhere. This behaviour has been observed in ravens in Bugnyar\'s captive studies and in corvids more broadly.' },
];
